"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
from teaching.foundations import put

put(5, '''Our payment program checks an amount, asks an outside service to charge the customer, and records the result. This outside service is the **payment provider**. An **object** combines data with operations on that data. A **class** describes how such objects are made. For example, each Payment object has its own identifier and status. ¶ The caller wants to request a charge. It should not need to assemble the provider's message fields. We give it an operation such as `gateway.charge(command)`. A **provider adapter** implements this operation: it translates the command into the provider's format. Offering the useful operation while hiding these details is **abstraction**. ¶ Now suppose any caller can set `payment.status = "paid"`. It could mark a payment paid without a successful charge. Instead, we control changes through an operation that checks the provider's confirmed result. This is **encapsulation**: controlling access to an object's data so its operations can protect the rules. A private field alone is insufficient if another method can change it without checking. ¶ Two gateways can offer the same charge operation. The service calls either in the same way, while each gateway handles a different provider. Using different implementations through the same operation is **polymorphism**. Both must give the caller compatible results. ¶ **Inheritance** creates a class from an existing class. A Receipt class might provide `amountText()`. A CardReceipt subclass inherits that operation and adds a card-reference display. It can reuse or replace inherited behaviour. Inheritance is one way to organise classes; the first three ideas do not require it. || Caller requests a charge / Gateway hides provider fields / Payment controls status changes / Either gateway serves the caller

Suppose our payment service can use two fraud policies and two payment providers. A fraud policy decides whether to allow a payment attempt. The provider carries out the charge. These are separate choices. ¶ If we encode every combination in subclasses, two policies and two providers can produce four service classes. Add another policy, and we need more combinations. With **composition**, the service holds a policy object and a gateway object. It asks the policy first, then uses the gateway. We can replace either part while keeping the other. ¶ Inheritance is useful when the new class can fulfil the promises of its parent. Suppose Account allows a withdrawal whenever enough money is available. A ReadOnlyAccount that always rejects withdrawal cannot safely replace it for callers relying on that promise. Similar fields do not establish a suitable parent–child relationship. ¶ Composition also needs clear operations between its parts. Use it to separate choices that really vary, rather than creating a new wrapper for every small calculation. || Choose one fraud policy / Choose one payment gateway / Service calls the supplied parts / Change either choice independently

What does `charge(100)` mean? Is it 100 dollars or 100 cents? The operation name leaves an important question unanswered. ¶ An **interface** describes the operations available to a caller. A **contract** states how those operations behave: which inputs are allowed, what the results mean, and what happens on failure. Our command includes `amount_minor=100`, `currency="USD"`, and `operation_id="P7"`. Here, a minor unit is a cent, so the amount is one US dollar. P7 identifies this intended charge. ¶ Our gateway returns three possible outcomes. “Approved” means the provider confirmed that the charge succeeded. “Declined” means it definitely rejected the charge. “Uncertain” means we do not yet know whether it succeeded. A provider timeout can produce the last outcome: it may have charged the customer before its reply was lost. ¶ The contract must also explain repeated commands. If the provider supports recognising P7, a retry with the same details can refer to the original charge. Adding the field ourselves does not create that support. We must use the provider's documented recovery method before deciding whether to try again. ¶ Every replacement gateway must preserve these meanings. Matching the method name and argument types is only part of the agreement. || Command: USD 100 minor units / P7 identifies the intended charge / Timeout leaves the result uncertain / Recover using the agreed procedure

A fee change should lead us to fee code. A receipt-layout change should lead us to receipt code. If both require editing one large payment method, their work has become tangled. ¶ **Cohesion** describes how closely the work inside a module belongs together. A module is an organised part of a program, such as a file or class. A fee module has high cohesion when its operations serve the same fee rules. A class containing fee arithmetic, image resizing, and email formatting has several unrelated jobs. ¶ **Coupling** describes dependencies between modules. If payment code builds provider-specific messages everywhere, a renamed provider field may require many edits. Put those field names inside the gateway adapter instead. The service still depends on the gateway's behaviour, but it needs less knowledge of the provider. ¶ The aim is a clear place for each change. Dividing one simple operation across many tiny classes can make it harder to follow, even when each class is short. || Fee policy calculates the fee / Receipt formatter builds the receipt / Adapter knows provider field names / Each change has a clear home

Let us add a second payment provider. **SOLID** names five principles that help us examine the design. ¶ **Single responsibility** says to keep work that changes for the same reason together. The finance team's fee rule and a provider's message format can change independently. Give them separate owners. One responsibility can still need several methods. ¶ **Open/closed** asks us to support a chosen kind of extension without rewriting working decisions. If the service already uses a gateway, we can add a provider adapter while keeping its payment logic. This protects that particular variation; a new business requirement may still require changing the service. ¶ **Liskov substitution** says that a replacement must preserve the behaviour promised to callers. A new gateway must keep the same amount units and outcome meanings. Reporting a timeout as a definite decline breaks that promise, even if the method signatures match. ¶ **Interface segregation** keeps a caller's interface focused on what it needs. Checkout needs charging; it should not need provider-reporting methods just to make a payment. ¶ **Dependency inversion** keeps payment rules from depending directly on a provider's software types. Define the gateway operation in terms the application owns, such as its ChargeCommand and ChargeResult. Each provider adapter implements that operation. At runtime the service still calls the adapter, but a provider-specific error code is translated before it reaches the payment rules. ¶ These principles do not require an interface for every class. Add structure where it helps a real change. Otherwise, extra layers can make the original operation harder to understand. || Keep fee and provider work separate / Add a compatible gateway adapter / Expose only needed operations / Keep provider types in the adapter

Suppose, by mistake, both provider adapters contain a copy of the business fee rule. The fee rises from 3 to 4 minor units, but we update only one adapter. Customers now pay different fees depending on the provider. The problem is two maintained copies of one rule. ¶ **DRY**, “Don't Repeat Yourself,” asks us to avoid duplicated knowledge. Put this shared fee rule in one function and let both adapters use it. Similar-looking code may still represent different rules: a customer-name limit and a receipt-title limit can change independently. Combining them just because both check text length would tie unrelated decisions together. ¶ **KISS**, commonly expanded as “Keep It Simple, Stupid,” asks for an understandable design. A direct fee function is easier to follow than a configurable rule engine when we need only one calculation. ¶ **YAGNI**, “You Aren't Gonna Need It,” asks us to defer features with no present need. We can add several fee strategies when the requirements call for them. We still implement the validation and failure handling that today's payment needs. || Two copies hold one fee rule / Only one copy gets updated / Move the shared rule to one function / Add variation when it is needed

Consider `order.customer.account.settings.currency`. To find one currency, the caller knows how four objects are arranged. If settings move, that caller must change even though it still needs the same answer. ¶ The order can instead offer `paymentCurrency()`. The order's code performs the lookup, and the caller depends on the meaning of that operation. Changing where the currency is stored no longer changes the caller. ¶ The **Law of Demeter** encourages a method to work with objects it knows directly, such as its own parts, its arguments, or objects it creates. It discourages reaching through several collaborators to use their internal parts. ¶ The benefit is less knowledge of internal structure, rather than fewer dots on a line. Some chains deliberately form a public interface, such as a builder that returns itself after each step. Add a forwarding operation when it hides a real representation choice; a chain of empty wrappers can obscure the work instead. || Caller follows four object links / Settings move to another place / Direct lookup code must change / paymentCurrency() hides that route

An account holds 1,000 minor units. A caller wants to reserve 600 for a payment. If every caller reads the balance, checks it, and edits the reserved amount, each must implement the same rule correctly. ¶ Instead, ask the account to `reserve(600)`. The account checks the available amount and updates its reservation together. After the first reservation, only 400 is available. Another reservation of 600 must fail. Keeping this rule beside the data is the idea behind **Tell, Don't Ask**: request an action from the object responsible for it. ¶ This does not ban queries. A screen can still ask for the available balance. The important difference is that the screen does not decide and write the reservation itself. ¶ Now let two processes both read 1,000 before either writes. Both separate checks can pass. The database must make the check and update **atomic**, so another writer cannot slip a conflicting change between them. For example, one conditional update can add 600 only if at least 600 remains available. An object method organises the rule; the storage operation enforces it across processes. || Available: 1000 minor units / First reservation: 600 / Remaining available: 400 / Second reservation of 600 fails

A screen calls `payment.status()` to display a payment. If that call also charges the customer, refreshing the screen could create another payment. A reader must be able to tell which calls request changes. ¶ A **query** returns information without changing the object's observable state. A **command** requests a change. Calling `status()` should read the status; calling `charge(command)` clearly asks for an effect. This distinction is **command-query separation**. ¶ Its strict form says that commands return no value. In practice, a command often returns a result or an operation identifier. State that convention clearly while keeping the requested effect explicit. A returned value alone does not make an operation a query. ¶ Two status reads can still differ if another operation changes the payment between them. Separating commands and queries does not freeze the system. **CQRS**, Command Query Responsibility Segregation, goes further by giving reads and changes separate models. Ordinary command-query separation needs no separate database or service. || status() reads the payment / charge() requests an effect / A command may report its result / Another command can change a later read

Our payment is 100 minor units and the fee is 3, both in USD. A function can calculate a total of `100 + 3 = 103` and return a new summary. The input amount need not change. ¶ An **immutable** value cannot be changed after it is created. To represent a different amount, we create another value. This lets us keep the original summary beside the revised one without wondering which helper edited it. ¶ A **pure function** returns the same result for the same inputs and changes nothing outside the calculation. Adding the amount and fee can be pure. Charging a provider is different: it creates an external **side effect**. Writing the result to a database is another effect. ¶ Keep the calculation and the effects easy to identify. The new summary does not itself charge anyone. An immutable charge command can still be sent twice, so immutability does not prevent duplicate charges. The code that performs the effect must still recognise the intended operation and recover from an uncertain reply. Very large values may also make copying expensive; measure that cost when choosing a representation. || Input: amount 100 and fee 3 / Pure calculation returns total 103 / Gateway performs the charge / Database records the observed result

Payment 42 begins as pending and later becomes paid. It is still payment 42. We care about its identity and history, so it is an **entity**. Another payment for the same amount is a different entity. ¶ Now consider `Money("USD", 100)`. Two such values mean the same amount and currency. We can replace one with an equal value without referring to a particular object's history. This is a **value object**. Money is often immutable: adding USD 3 produces a new USD 103 value instead of changing USD 100 in place. ¶ These choices are separate. An entity can also have immutable snapshots: the pending and paid snapshots share identifier 42. The identifier connects them as states of the same payment. ¶ A **domain model** expresses the concepts and rules of the problem we are solving. Here, Money describes amounts and Payment describes a payment's progress. A rule that must always hold for a valid object is an **invariant**. For our Payment, a paid status must be backed by a confirmed successful charge. Money addition has a separate input requirement: the currencies must match. Adding USD to EUR needs an explicit conversion rule first. ¶ Some business calculations involve several objects and fit no single entity. A **domain service** can hold such a rule, such as choosing a transfer fee from the sender, recipient, and amount. Provider message formatting remains separate from that business calculation. || Payment 42 keeps its identifier / Its status changes over time / Equal Money values are interchangeable / Money rules reject mixed-currency addition

Checkout needs an inventory reservation and a payment. The checkout coordinator asks inventory to reserve the items. Inventory checks stock and returns reservation R8. Checkout records R8 so it can refer to that exact reservation later. ¶ Next, checkout asks the payment service to charge under operation P7. If the charge is confirmed, checkout can mark the order paid. If it is definitely declined, checkout asks inventory to release R8. Each object owns its rules, while the coordinator keeps track of the sequence. This exchange of requests and results is **object collaboration**. ¶ A timeout needs a different next step. P7 may already have charged, so checkout keeps the result pending and seeks the provider's recorded outcome. Returning an error does not undo the reservation or the charge. Releasing a reservation or refunding a charge is another operation whose result must also be tracked. ¶ These collaborators may run inside one program. Moving them into separate services adds network waits and failures; a method call inside a process does not account for those by itself. || Inventory reserves items as R8 / Checkout requests payment P7 / Decline leads to release of R8 / Uncertainty keeps P7 under investigation

How can we test a declined payment without charging a real customer? Supply a **test double**, a small replacement gateway whose reply we control. Let it return “declined,” then call the service normally. ¶ Check the result the customer depends on: the payment is recorded as declined, the order is not paid, and shipment has not begun. Repeat with “approved” and “uncertain.” The uncertain case should leave the payment pending, rather than inventing a definite failure. ¶ Passing the gateway into the service, instead of constructing it inside the payment method, is **dependency injection**. It gives the test a place to supply its replacement. The same arrangement lets production code supply a real provider adapter. ¶ A test that checks only private method names may fail after harmless code movement while missing a wrong final status. Check meaningful results and required effects. Tests using the real adapter and storage are still needed for message encoding, database commits, and actual provider behaviour. A fake reply cannot establish that the real provider recognises repeated operation identifiers. || Supply a gateway returning declined / Run the payment operation / Check status and shipment outcome / Check real boundaries separately

Our large payment method mixes fee calculation, provider messages, and result storage. We want to separate these jobs while keeping the same behaviour for callers. Such a change is **refactoring**. ¶ First record examples of approval, decline, timeout, and storage failure. For each, state the returned result and the resulting payment state. Then extract the fee calculation into a function. Run the same examples and compare the amounts. Next extract provider translation, keeping the outcome meanings unchanged. Check again after that step. ¶ A **code smell** is a clue that some code deserves attention: perhaps one fee rule appears in three places, or adding a provider keeps expanding the same conditional. It is not proof of a bug. The useful repair depends on the difficulty. A duplicated fee rule suggests one shared definition; an awkward name may need only a rename. ¶ If the old code wrongly treats timeout as decline, correcting it changes behaviour. Make that correction explicit and test it as a separate change. Calling it a refactor would hide the new promise from reviewers. || Record current success and failure results / Extract the fee calculation / Check the same amounts and outcomes / Extract the adapter and check again

We can represent payment decisions with functions as well as objects. Consider `decide(state, result)`. Give it a pending payment and the observed provider result. For “approved,” it returns a paid state. For “declined,” it returns a declined state. For “uncertain,” it leaves the payment pending and requests a later status check. ¶ This decision can be a pure function. It makes no provider call and writes no database row. The same state and result produce the same decision, so a test can check the output directly. ¶ Another part of the program performs the requested work. It records progress, calls the provider when needed, and passes the observed result into the decision function. This arrangement is often called a **functional core with an imperative shell**: pure decisions at the centre, code that performs effects around them. ¶ Replaying the decision is different from repeating a charge. The outer code must keep track of which external operations have already run. A pure function makes the decision easier to inspect; it does not make a remote charge and a local database write happen as one operation. Functions and objects can be combined wherever each makes the rules clearer. || Input: pending state and provider result / Pure function chooses the next state / Outer code records or performs work / Track effects before replaying decisions''',
['Four ways to organise an object',
 'Build with parts or derive a class?',
 'State exactly what a charge means',
 'Keep related work together',
 'Use SOLID to examine a real change',
 'Keep one copy of a shared rule',
 'Avoid knowing the whole object chain',
 'Ask the owner to perform the change',
 'Make reads and changes easy to distinguish',
 'Calculate new values, then perform effects',
 'Distinguish identity from value',
 'Follow the conversation between objects',
 'Test decisions with a replaceable gateway',
 'Improve the structure one step at a time',
 'Express decisions as functions'])

put(6, '''Our notification service sends an email when an order is ready. It uses an email sender to do this work. The sender is a **dependency**: another part of the program that the service needs. If the service builds requests using a provider's software library, changing that library may force us to change the service too. ¶ We can first write down what the service needs from any sender. It supplies a recipient and message, and receives an outcome such as “provider accepted” or “rejected.” This agreement is a **contract**. Acceptance means the provider accepted the request; it does not prove that the person read the message. ¶ Call this contract `Sender`. An email adapter translates it into the provider's request and translates the reply back. The service's code now names `Sender`; the adapter's code names both `Sender` and the provider library. This is the direction of their source dependencies. When the program runs, the service still calls the actual email adapter. Which code knows which types, and which object runs next, are separate questions. || Service needs a sender / Sender states message and outcomes / Adapter translates provider details / Runtime call still reaches adapter

In a small script, we write `send_receipt()` in the main function and decide when it runs. In a web application, we register a function to handle a request. The framework calls that function when a matching request arrives. ¶ Our function supplies the notification behaviour. The framework controls when to call it. This arrangement is **Inversion of Control**, or **IoC**. Control of part of the execution has moved from our code to the surrounding program. A registered function called later is a **callback**. ¶ This lets many handlers use the framework's request handling without writing their own server loop. It also gives the handler a boundary to follow: when is its work finished, how are errors reported, and who closes resources? Starting work that continues after the handler returns needs a separate owner. A framework can also supply objects to the handler. We will examine that particular technique next. || Register request handler / Request reaches framework / Framework calls the handler / Handler returns its outcome

Suppose `NotificationService` constructs an `EmailSender` inside itself. The service has chosen its own provider. A test that only wants to inspect a message must now deal with that choice. ¶ Move the construction to startup. Create `sender = EmailSender(config)`, then pass it into `NotificationService(sender)`. Supplying a dependency from outside is **Dependency Injection**, or **DI**. The service keeps the supplied sender and calls its `send` operation when needed. ¶ For a test, pass a `RecordingSender` instead. It records messages in memory without contacting a provider. The test can check the recipient and text using the same service code. Both senders must follow the agreed contract. ¶ Ordinary constructors and function arguments are enough for DI. A container is optional. The useful change is that the service receives its sender instead of choosing and building it during its own work. || Startup creates EmailSender / Startup passes it to the service / Service uses the supplied sender / Test supplies RecordingSender

Our service always needs a sender. With **constructor injection**, `NotificationService(sender)` receives it as the object is created. We make the constructor reject a missing sender, so a successfully created service is ready to send. ¶ An optional component that records send outcomes can be supplied later through `set_observer(observer)`. This is **setter injection**; assigning a property serves a similar purpose. Until one is supplied, the service can skip observations. If the dependency were mandatory, calls would need to be prevented until it was set. Otherwise, the object could exist without being usable. ¶ With **method injection**, an operation receives what it needs for that call: `render(message, formatter)`. The next call can use another formatter. The method need not keep it. ¶ These forms answer when the dependency arrives. They also affect how long it is retained. A service may use a request's database session during that request, but storing it for later calls would outlive its valid use. || Constructor supplies required sender / Setter supplies optional observer / Method supplies one-call formatter / Retain only while use is valid

Consider `NotificationService(provider_sdk)`. The provider's software library, or **SDK**, has been passed in, so this is DI. But the service may still construct that SDK's special request objects. A provider change can still require a service change. ¶ The **Dependency Inversion Principle**, or **DIP**, concerns this knowledge. Important application rules should depend on suitable contracts rather than on the details that carry them out. Our notification rule needs “send this message,” so it depends on `Sender`. The email adapter implements that contract and handles the SDK's details. ¶ The service and adapter now both refer to the application's contract. The provider can change while the notification rule stays the same, provided the new adapter can satisfy that contract. Passing the adapter into the service combines DI with this dependency direction. ¶ Inspect the values and errors too. Returning a provider-specific response for the service to interpret would carry those details back across the boundary. || Injecting an SDK supplies it / Service may still know SDK details / Define application Sender contract / Adapter handles provider translation

Before requests arrive, startup code creates an email sender and a notification service. It passes the sender to the service, then gives the service to the request handler. The place where concrete objects are chosen and connected is the **composition root**. ¶ This makes the setup easy to inspect. We can see which provider is used and whether two services share the same sender. The service itself can concentrate on sending notifications. ¶ A **DI container** automates some assembly. We register a rule such as “to obtain Sender, construct EmailSender.” When asked for a service, the container follows its construction rules to obtain that service's dependencies. We also tell it when to reuse objects. ¶ These rules are part of the program. Check that required registrations exist before serving traffic, and define who closes any created resources. For a small application, a few explicit constructor calls may be clearer than container configuration. || Startup selects concrete classes / Construct sender and service / Pass service to the handler / Container can automate this assembly

Two parts of request A need a database session. We want them to share one session, while request B gets another. With a **scoped** lifetime, the container reuses one instance within a **scope**, a boundary such as one request. Here the results are A1, A1 inside request A and B1, B1 inside request B. ¶ With a **transient** lifetime, each request to the container for that dependency creates a new instance. Two such lookups inside request A can return T1 and T2. With a **singleton** lifetime, lookups reuse one instance within the container boundary. Both requests can receive S. ¶ These names describe reuse. They do not by themselves tell us whether a shared object is safe for concurrent calls. A second process can also have its own S. Check the framework's actual boundaries and cleanup rules: “one” must mean one within a defined place, and “scoped” must name a scope. || Request A gets A1 then A1 / Request B gets B1 then B1 / Transient lookups get T1 then T2 / Singleton lookups reuse S

A request opens a database session and passes it to a background notification job. The response ends and the request closes the session. When the job later tries a query, its reference points to a closed resource. ¶ Give the job its own **scope**: a defined period during which its objects may be reused and must remain valid. The request saves the notification data and queues its identifier. The worker opens a job scope, loads the notification using a current session, does the work, and closes that scope. ¶ A request scope covers one request. A session scope, where supported, may cover several requests in a user's session. A custom scope can cover a job. Frameworks define these boundaries differently, so the start and end must be explicit. ¶ Keeping an object for a longer time also keeps its memory and user data. A scope does not automatically start a database transaction or keep a user's permission current. || Request creates notification job / Request closes its own session / Worker opens a fresh job scope / Worker closes resources when done

A factory returns a stream. Must the caller close it? The answer depends on **ownership**: responsibility for releasing the resource. ¶ The factory may create a fresh stream and transfer ownership to the caller. The caller then closes it after use. Or it may return an application-owned shared sender. The caller borrows that sender and leaves it open; its owner closes it at shutdown. Creating an object and owning its cleanup are separate decisions. ¶ The return contract should state which case applies. Otherwise, one caller may close a resource another still needs, or everybody may assume somebody else will close it. ¶ Some returned objects still use resources behind them. A result that fetches database rows as we read it needs its connection until reading ends. Either return it with clear close rules, or fetch a bounded set of records before closing the connection and returning those records. || Factory creates or finds resource / Contract names its owner / Borrower uses it within that lifetime / Owner releases it after final use

A pool has ten database connections. A request takes one, fails, and never returns it. Only nine remain available. After enough such failures, a healthy request cannot begin because all ten are still held. ¶ Place acquisition and release in one structured resource block, such as Python's `with`, or use a `finally` block. On normal completion or a handled exception, the block releases what it owns. Cancellation also needs a path that runs this cleanup. A forced process stop cannot be assumed to run language cleanup code. ¶ Cleanup may close a stream, return a connection, or release a permit. An unfinished transaction may need rollback before its connection can be reused. Releasing resources preserves capacity for the next operation. ¶ Garbage collection reclaims memory under runtime rules. It gives no timely-release guarantee for a scarce database connection. Follow the resource's cleanup contract even when its object will eventually become unreachable. || Take one pooled connection / Operation succeeds or fails / Exit runs the owning cleanup path / Connection becomes available again

Compiling a report template is expensive, and many runs never produce a report. We can wait until the first report request to create its generator. This is **lazy initialisation**. It avoids unused startup work but makes the first report wait for construction. ¶ Suppose two first requests both see “no generator.” Without coordination, both may construct one. Use the runtime's supported lazy facility or a suitable lock to make the intended check-and-create sequence safe. Other callers must receive a fully initialised object, rather than a reference published halfway through construction. ¶ Construction can fail too. Decide whether later callers retry or receive the saved failure, and release resources left by an unsuccessful attempt. If every ordinary request needs this object, creating it before the application reports readiness can reveal a configuration problem earlier. Laziness changes when the work happens; it does not remove the work or its possible failure. || First report needs the generator / Coordinate the check and creation / Share only a completed object / Define what follows construction failure

To construct `NotificationService`, we need `DeliveryPlanner`. But the planner's constructor needs the notification service. Neither can be completed first. These constructor requirements form a **dependency cycle**. ¶ Find why both links exist. Perhaps each class does work that belongs in a separate coordinator, or both need the same smaller helper. Moving that work can remove a link. A later global lookup may merely postpone the problem. ¶ Now consider a different failure. An application-wide service stores the database session from request A. Request A ends and closes its session. The service remains and tries to use that session during request B. A longer-lived object has kept a dependency beyond its intended scope. This is a **captive dependency**. ¶ Supply a currently valid session for each operation, or inject a narrow factory that opens and closes an operation scope. The operation must finish before that scope closes. Extending every session indefinitely would replace the original problem with uncontrolled sharing. || Constructor cycle prevents assembly / Inspect why each link is needed / Long-lived service captures request session / Next request reaches a closed session

Compare `NotificationService(sender)` with a service that calls `registry.resolve(Sender)` inside its methods. The first constructor tells us that a sender is needed. For the second service, we must read the methods to discover that need. ¶ An object used to find other services this way is a **service locator**. A missing registration may appear only when the lookup branch runs. Tests may also need shared registry setup before they can call the service. ¶ With DI, assembly code obtains the sender and supplies it explicitly. Tests can supply their own sender directly. Passing the entire container into the constructor still leaves hidden needs if the service uses it to look up arbitrary objects. ¶ A framework integration point may need container access. Keep that lookup at the assembly or scope boundary, then pass the specific collaborators into the application code. This makes the code's needs easier to see and limits what it can obtain. || Constructor names the sender / Locator hides lookup inside a method / Missing lookup fails on that path / Assembly supplies focused dependencies

Our service once sent only email. Startup could create its sender immediately. Now each notification specifies email or SMS, so the channel is not known until the notification arrives. ¶ Give the service a `SenderFactory`. Its operation `for_channel(channel)` returns a suitable sender. Startup injects the factory; during a call, the service asks it for the chosen channel. **DI** supplies the factory, while the **factory** makes the later selection or construction decision. ¶ A factory does not always create a fresh object. It can return a shared sender already built at startup. Its contract must say whether the returned sender is shared, borrowed from a scope, or newly owned by the caller. This determines whether the service may close or retain it. ¶ Keep the operation narrow. The need to select a sender does not require giving the service permission to obtain every object in the application. || Startup injects SenderFactory / Notification specifies SMS / Factory returns the SMS sender / Caller follows its ownership rule

A notification has a recipient, a message, and optional settings. A constructor can often check these values and return a usable object. Start there. ¶ More complex creation raises different questions. Should a subclass select a writer? Must a renderer and template come from the same family? Are we collecting settings over several steps, copying a configured object, or restricting access to one instance? ¶ **Creational patterns** name arrangements for these needs. The next five examples follow the construction step by step. The choice depends on what must vary. A pattern is useful when it makes that choice clearer; it should not make a simple constructor harder to follow. || Start with a direct constructor / Identify the construction problem / Choose an arrangement for that problem / Return a valid usable object

Our service has a sender. We may need to translate an old provider's interface, measure each send, or check access before calling it. Each change can place another object around the sender, but the purpose differs. ¶ **Structural patterns** describe arrangements like these. Some translate between interfaces. Some group objects or add behaviour around an operation. Others simplify access or share repeated data. ¶ For each example below, follow the call: what does the added object receive, what does it do, and what does it pass onward? This reveals the pattern more clearly than the wrapper's shape. A timing wrapper and a provider translator can look similar in a diagram while making different promises to their callers. || Begin with an existing operation / Add an object for a specific purpose / Follow what it does before forwarding / Check the meaning of its result

A notification service must choose how to render a message, tell interested listeners that it was accepted, and permit actions that fit its current status. These are different decisions. ¶ **Behavioural patterns** help organise such decisions and the calls between objects. The examples below show where a chosen algorithm lives, how listeners are called, how an action is represented, and how an object's history changes its permitted actions. ¶ Some patterns are well expressed with functions or small tables. Others benefit from separate objects. Follow the changing rule first, then choose a representation that makes that rule easy to read. Adding a class for every decision is not a requirement. || Identify the changing behaviour / Put its rule in a clear place / Trace calls and state changes / Use functions or objects as needed

Accepting a notification may mean saving both its message and its delivery job. The application also needs rules about which notifications are valid, and a screen needs to list their progress. ¶ These needs lead to different boundaries. A **Repository** supplies application-level data operations. A **Specification** expresses a rule as a test. A **Unit of Work** tracks related changes so they can be saved together. **CQRS** separates the models used to change data and to read it when their needs differ. ¶ We will examine each below. They can all be used within one application and one database. What matters is which work each part owns. In particular, saving related database changes together does not make an email provider part of that same transaction. || Find and save application data / Express a rule clearly / Group related database changes / Shape reads for their actual needs''',
['See what the service needs',
 'Let another part of the program make the call',
 'Pass the sender into the service',
 'Supply a dependency when it becomes necessary',
 'Separate injection from dependency inversion',
 'Assemble the application in one clear place',
 'Choose when objects are reused',
 'Give each request or job its own boundary',
 'Decide who must close the resource',
 'Release resources on every exit path',
 'Create an expensive object only when needed',
 'Find cycles and dependencies kept too long',
 'Show dependencies instead of looking them up inside',
 'Inject a factory for a choice made later',
 'Choose how an object is created',
 'Arrange objects around an existing operation',
 'Give each behaviour a clear home',
 'Separate data access, rules, and commit decisions'])

put(7, '''A shopping request arrives through a controller, invokes an application operation, applies pricing rules, and stores the order. Layered architecture gives these different kinds of work clear places: presentation, application coordination, domain policy, and infrastructure. ¶ The layers can all run in one process. Deploying them as separate physical tiers is another choice. Use the division to protect useful rules: a controller should not bypass order validation simply because it can reach an SQL helper. Follow a change, such as a new pricing rule, to see whether the boundary helps. Classes that only forward every call add work without necessarily hiding any detail. || Controller parses input / Application coordinates use case / Domain enforces rule / Infrastructure implements persistence

Catalog, orders, and payments can be modules of one deployable application. Each exposes operations and owns its data, while calls between them remain local. This is a modular monolith. ¶ The word “modular” matters. If every module writes every table, changing a payment record can still break the whole program. Use agreed interfaces and enforce dependency rules through review or useful automated checks. This arrangement keeps deployment and local transactions relatively simple while giving business responsibilities separate homes. Later extraction remains possible, but it is an option supported by the boundaries, rather than a benefit obtained automatically by naming folders. || One deployment / Catalog owns product representation / Orders owns purchase lifecycle / Payments owns payment outcomes

Suppose inventory needs to scale independently and has a team ready to operate it. Making it a separate service may provide a useful deployment and ownership boundary. It also replaces some local calls with network calls that can time out or partially fail. ¶ Microservices aim for useful independent deployment and operation. A monolith can be well organised, while several services can remain tightly coupled. If five services must release together for each change, ask how much independence was gained. Compare the motivation for extraction with the added work in data coordination, compatibility, monitoring, and recovery. A process boundary should solve a demonstrated problem. || Modular application / Identify independent operating need / Extract one stable boundary / Accept network and consistency costs

An order rule should be testable without constructing an HTTP response or starting a web framework. Clean Architecture supports this by making framework and storage details depend on interfaces used by the application's policy. ¶ The inward arrows in its usual diagrams describe source dependencies. During execution, a use case can still call an outer storage adapter through an injected interface. Avoid making core rules depend on ORM-specific records or transport response objects. The separation costs mappings and contracts, so choose a proportionate version for a small application. The important question is whether policy can be understood and exercised without unrelated infrastructure details. || Domain policy at center / Use case depends on port / Adapter implements port / Runtime call crosses outward through contract

An order can be created from a web request or a command-line tool. Both adapters can invoke the same application operation. Likewise, an SQL adapter and a test repository can satisfy the application's storage needs. ¶ Hexagonal architecture calls these application-facing contracts ports and their implementations adapters. An inbound port expresses an operation the application offers; an outbound port expresses work it needs from elsewhere. Design ports around those needs rather than copying an entire vendor API. The drawing does not imply separate machines. Test application decisions with controlled adapters, then separately check real encoding, transactions, and failure behaviour at the external boundaries. || HTTP adapter to inbound port / CLI adapter to same port / Use case invokes outbound port / SQL adapter implements persistence

Onion Architecture arranges domain policy, application services, and infrastructure so that dependencies point toward the core rules. An order rule can then use an application-owned repository contract rather than import database-specific code. ¶ Its vocabulary overlaps with Clean and Hexagonal Architecture, and teams draw the layers differently. Compare the actual dependency and ownership rules instead of treating the shape as a guarantee. Can pricing run without a web server? Does a failed write remain visible to the caller? These questions reveal whether the separation helps. Extra layers are worthwhile when they protect such properties, rather than simply matching a diagram. || Domain rule / Application coordination / Infrastructure implementation / Dependencies preserve policy independence

Ask a shop's merchandising and warehouse staff what “available stock” means. Merchandising may mean stock that can be advertised. A reservation worker may mean units that can still be promised to a buyer. Treating both as one unexamined field can produce wrong decisions. ¶ Domain-Driven Design develops a model with the people who understand the business. The model names concepts and makes their rules explicit. Use this effort where business meaning is complex or disputed. A simple lookup table may need only straightforward data handling. DDD's value comes from clearer decisions and shared understanding, rather than assigning a class to every database row. || Talk through a business scenario / Identify ambiguous terms / Name rules and invariants / Model the behavior together

Catalog's Product may describe a title, images, and marketing categories. Fulfilment's Item may describe a physical unit and its shelf location. Both models can be correct for their own work. ¶ A bounded context states where a model and its vocabulary apply. Within it, a ubiquitous language gives team members and code consistent names for the business concepts. Between contexts, translate deliberately and identify who owns each fact. One universal Product schema may obscure real differences. A context is a modelling boundary; deciding whether it becomes a separate service, repository, or team requires further reasons. || Catalog Product model / Translation boundary / Fulfillment Item model / Each context owns its meaning

An order's line items and total must agree when it is committed. We can give an Order root responsibility for changing those related values together. An aggregate is this kind of consistency boundary, with a root controlling changes to its members. ¶ Entities have identity through change; value objects are distinguished by their values. A domain service can express a rule that does not belong to one entity. Keep an aggregate focused on invariants that need a common atomic change. Putting the entire shop into one aggregate creates contention and loading costs. Rules spanning aggregates need an explicit coordination design; an object reference alone does not create a distributed transaction. || Command reaches aggregate root / Root validates local invariant / Members change atomically / Cross-aggregate work needs a protocol

After an order is accepted, other parts of the system may need to know. OrderPlaced records a business fact. Give it a stable identity, clear time meaning, and information that its intended consumers can interpret across versions. ¶ Creating an event object in memory is not the same as publishing it reliably. The process may commit the order and die before sending the event. An outbox records order state and publication intent in one local transaction, then relays the event. Consumers still need to handle repeated and delayed delivery. Define a fact's meaning before deciding how the broker will carry it. || Order transition commits / OrderPlaced intent persisted / Relay publishes event / Consumers deduplicate and interpret version

Suppose the orders module updates a payment table directly. It now knows the payment representation and can bypass payment rules. A payment interface gives that interaction a controlled place. ¶ Dependency rules specify which modules may know about or call others. Enforce important boundaries with appropriate import checks, database permissions, or architecture tests. A cycle can reveal that coordination has no clear owner. Shared helpers also need scrutiny: one “common model” can accidentally force unrelated contexts to change together. Where meanings genuinely differ, use an explicit translation rather than sharing a type merely because its fields currently look similar. || Orders uses payment contract / Payments owns payment tables / Direct table write is rejected / Boundary checked in source and storage

“A refunded order must not ship” is a business rule. Asking the payment service for a refund and then updating progress is orchestration. Encoding and sending a provider request is infrastructure work. ¶ These responsibilities may begin in one function, but distinguishing them helps assign tests and handle failures. Test the shipping rule as a decision. Test orchestration across success and partial failure. Test the provider adapter against its actual protocol. Moving a sequence onto a queue does not make its business rules belong to the broker. Keep the owner of each decision visible even when the execution arrangement changes. || Policy decides allowed transition / Orchestrator requests effects / Adapter communicates externally / Result returns to explicit state machine

A controller reads a request and builds a response. An application service coordinates an operation. A repository offers data operations, and an adapter connects a contract to a particular protocol or technology. ¶ These are roles, not required class-name endings. A service can be a function; a domain service is not necessarily a network service. Examine the work the code performs. If a controller calculates prices and retries transactions, creating an empty “services” directory has not moved those responsibilities. Choose names and boundaries that help readers follow one real operation from its input to its result. || Transport data to command / Application use case / Domain-oriented data access / Boundary-specific implementation

A legacy payment provider may use “settled” for an authorisation that our application still considers pending. Copying that label directly would import the provider's meaning into our own model. ¶ An anti-corruption layer translates the external model into the local one. It maps known states, retains useful provider references, and handles unknown meanings explicitly. This adds mapping and maintenance work, but keeps external quirks from spreading through business code. Never turn an unmapped result silently into success. Where the provider's state is uncertain or different from ours, preserve enough information to investigate and reconcile it. || Legacy provider state / Explicit semantic translation / Local payment state / Unknown state remains an actionable error

An accepted order can trigger a receipt without making checkout wait for email delivery. Event-driven architecture allows components to react independently to recorded events. This can remove a synchronous dependency from the user's path. ¶ Decide which steps may actually be delayed. Inventory and payment requirements may require stronger coordination than receipts. Event processing also adds delivery, ordering, replay, and monitoring work. Name the owner of each event and state how it is persisted, recognised on redelivery, and recovered after a failure. A broker transports messages; the application must still arrange for the required business outcome. || Durable order event / Broker stores or routes message / Consumer updates its state / Reconciliation detects incomplete outcomes

A cloud function can process an uploaded file when an event arrives. The platform manages invocation and some execution capacity, but the function still has deadlines, concurrency limits, credentials, and possible retries. ¶ Treat function-local memory as temporary unless the platform guarantees otherwise. Store required progress durably, and recognise repeated invocations before applying repeated effects. Small independently triggered tasks can fit this arrangement well. A long sequence with human review or remote uncertainty needs a durable owner for its state, often a workflow engine. “Serverless” changes operating responsibilities; it leaves application correctness and recovery decisions to be designed. || Event invokes function / Function receives bounded execution / External state persists outcome / Retry may invoke another instance

The application must keep tenant data isolated while releases change its internals. A check that attempts a cross-tenant read can protect that property during development. Similar checks can protect module boundaries, migration compatibility, or a latency budget. ¶ Evolutionary architecture uses such feedback to change a system while preserving important qualities. A fitness check should express a useful property, rather than freeze an incidental class layout. Record why a choice exists and when evidence should prompt reconsideration. Evolution may involve removing an unnecessary service or simplifying a contract. It is a process of guided change, not an obligation to replace the architecture repeatedly. || Change is proposed / Fitness checks test required properties / Evidence supports a reversible step / Decision rationale evolves with workload

Controllers and database adapters are technical groupings. Orders and fulfilment are business capabilities. The two kinds of division can coexist: each business module can have its own internal input, policy, and storage code. ¶ Making separate network services for “validation” and “SQL” may force every business operation to call both. Begin with ownership of decisions and facts, then choose the technical arrangement inside those boundaries. A bounded context describes a model's scope. It does not automatically dictate a deployment or team structure. Follow actual business changes to see which parts need to evolve together. || Business capability owns decisions / Internal layers implement mechanics / External contract hides representation / Deployment follows an actual need

Five services share writable tables and must be released together. Most requests synchronously pass through all five. Although deployed separately, they cannot change or fail independently. This is commonly called a distributed monolith. ¶ Network boundaries now add timeouts and debugging work without delivering the expected independence. Clarify data ownership and contracts before promising independent services. Sometimes consolidating unstable boundaries into a modular application is the easier improvement. In other cases, redesigning the interactions can justify the separation. Judge progress by independent behaviour and manageable failures, rather than the number of deployed processes. || Several services share schema / Every release deploys all services / One dependency stalls the chain / Clarify ownership or consolidate boundary

A small team is still discovering how orders and fulfilment should work. Splitting them into many services immediately would add contract and operating work while the boundaries are changing. A modular monolith can provide a simpler starting point. ¶ Consider extraction when a stable capability has distinct scaling, isolation, or ownership needs and the team can operate it. Include distributed transactions, version compatibility, telemetry, security, and incident response in the cost. Neither low traffic nor a service count settles the choice. Prefer the design that meets the actual requirements and can be changed and recovered by the people responsible for it. || Small team and changing model / Modular deployment satisfies workload / Measure concrete scaling or ownership pain / Extract only a justified capability''')

put(8, '''A client calls create-payment. It needs to know the amount units, currency, permitted caller, returned states, and what to do if no reply arrives. A field schema alone cannot tell it whether retrying might charge twice. ¶ An API is an interface together with a communication contract. Describe inputs, results, effects, errors, timing, operation identity, and supported evolution. Begin with concrete histories: successful creation, invalid input, a repeated request, and a lost reply after submission. These expose decisions a transport framework cannot make for you. Keep a definite provider decline distinct from an operation whose outcome is still uncertain. || Client sends payment intent / Server validates identity and shape / Operation has explicit side effect / Result defines retry and uncertainty

The same payment capability can be exposed in several ways. REST emphasises resources and HTTP semantics. GraphQL lets clients query a typed graph. gRPC commonly supplies schema-defined remote calls and streaming. RPC more generally models operations called across a boundary. ¶ Choose by what clients need, how the API will evolve, and the cost of serving it. GraphQL can reduce unwanted data transfer but needs query-cost and field-access controls. An RPC name can make an action clear while leaving retries and compatibility to be specified. Compare complete operation histories and tooling, rather than assuming a request syntax establishes reliability or performance. || Same payment business operation / Resource API or operation API / Schema and authorization obligations / Transport does not decide correctness

A payment and its refund are related, but they have different identities and lifecycles. A refund may be requested later, authorised separately, and succeed or fail independently. ¶ One resource model uses POST /payments, GET /payments/{id}, and POST /payments/{id}/refunds. The refund then has its own amount, status, and operation identifier. Resource modelling gives these continuing business objects clear identities. Avoid a generic /execute endpoint that hides every meaning, while also avoiding a forced CRUD model that makes domain actions unclear. Let ownership and allowed state transitions guide the interface. || Payment resource ID / Independent refund resource ID / Refund references original payment / Both have explicit status transitions

A request can contain valid JSON yet ask to charge a negative amount. Parsing establishes structural validity; application validation checks business meaning. Check both. ¶ Bound body size, collection length, and supported encodings before expensive work. Decide how unknown fields are handled so clients can evolve predictably. Return a stable machine-readable error code with a useful, safe message and a request identifier. Avoid exposing secrets or raw provider internals. Consider strict clients as well as tolerant ones: a new field can affect a client that rejects unknown input. The schema and its compatibility policy belong together. || Bound request bytes / Parse declared schema / Validate business invariant / Return stable safe error model

The server returns 201 after creating a payment resource whose status is pending. The resource exists; money has not necessarily been captured. Similarly, 202 indicates acceptance for processing rather than final approval. ¶ HTTP status describes the protocol response, while the body can describe the business operation's state. A timeout supplies neither a definitive approval nor a decline. Use application error codes to distinguish invalid input, conflicts, denied access, and uncertain submitted work. This lets clients choose an appropriate next action. Turning every provider problem into the same generic error can hide whether the caller should correct input, wait, or query an existing operation. || HTTP request accepted / Payment resource is pending / Later business outcome arrives / Client uses status endpoint to reconcile

A request for every payment can return too much data and consume too much memory. Pagination divides the collection into bounded responses. Define maximum page size, permitted filters, ordering, and access checks. ¶ Sorting only by creation time can leave rows with equal timestamps in an unstable order. Add a unique tie-breaker such as the payment identifier. Decide what happens while the collection changes between page requests. Neither offset nor cursor pagination automatically gives the client one frozen snapshot. Enforce tenant access in the query itself, rather than retrieving a large shared collection and hoping the client filters it correctly. || Filter authorized records / Sort by time then unique ID / Apply bounded page size / Return continuation metadata

Suppose a client reads the first ten rows, then asks to skip ten. A new row inserted near the beginning shifts positions, so the second page may repeat or omit an item. This is a difficulty with offset pagination on changing data. ¶ A keyset cursor instead records the last ordered key, such as (created_at,id), and continues after that key. It can reduce skipping cost and these shifting-position problems. Updates to existing rows still need a stated consistency policy. Validate opaque cursors or protect their integrity, and bind them to the applicable filters and tenant. A cursor must not become a way to escape the original query's access rules. || Page ends at key (100,42) / New row inserted before that key / Continue after (100,42) / Cursor remains bound to query and tenant

A payment amount changes from cents to dollars while its JSON type remains numeric. Old clients can now submit a hundred times the intended amount. Compatibility concerns meaning as well as syntax. ¶ Adding an optional field is safer for tolerant clients than changing an existing field's meaning, but strict validators and enum handling may still break. Use an explicit version policy, an overlap period, and evidence about old consumers. Test representative clients. A versioned URL is one tool; it does not migrate callers or update stored events. Plan the transition for every reader that still relies on the earlier contract. || Existing client contract / Proposed semantic change / Compatibility test and migration window / Retire old behavior with evidence

A client retries a payment after losing its reply. It sends the same idempotency key so the server can recognise the original logical operation. The server must bind that key to the authenticated owner, request content, progress, and result. ¶ Enforce uniqueness and the required atomic updates in storage. If the same key arrives with a different amount, return a conflict instead of replaying an unrelated result. Define how long the key is remembered. A provider timeout may require querying or reconciling that provider before another submission. Local key storage alone cannot promise one external charge if the provider does not support the necessary operation identity and recovery behaviour. || Key K and payload hash H / Atomically reserve operation K / Execute or reconcile provider operation / Persist and replay the same result

A user signs in successfully, then requests payment 42. The server still needs to check whether that user owns or may view payment 42. Knowing an identity and granting an action are separate decisions. ¶ Authentication establishes identity under the chosen trust mechanism. Authorisation checks permission for this action on this resource. Derive tenant context from validated membership rather than trusting an arbitrary tenant_id field. A gateway can perform coarse checks, but the service with ownership information must enforce the resource rule. Apply appropriate identity checks to internal service callers too. An obscure identifier reduces guessing; it is not an access-control mechanism. || Validate credential / Resolve permitted tenant and roles / Load resource under tenant constraint / Authorize the requested action

A client makes too many expensive validation requests at once. A rate limit controls that pace, often with a specified burst allowance. A daily export quota instead caps accumulated use over a longer period or resource dimension. ¶ State the identity being limited, window, cost unit, retry guidance, and behaviour if the limiter fails. Per-tenant controls can protect neighbours from one noisy client. Per-user limits alone may miss coordinated abuse. Across regions, a strict global limit needs shared coordination or safely allocated local budgets. If enforcement is approximate, specify the approximation rather than silently giving every region the full global allowance. || Identify tenant and operation / Check burst and sustained allowance / Admit or reject with retry guidance / Account for distributed enforcement scope

A payment request has an overall deadline. Connection acquisition, each attempt, and any backoff must fit inside it. Passing only a fresh full timeout to every downstream call can make the total wait exceed the client's intended limit. ¶ Retry transient work only where repetition is safe. Exponential backoff with jitter spreads attempts but does not add capacity. If four layers each allow three attempts, one intent can cause up to 3⁴ = 81 downstream attempts. Choose a shared retry budget and propagate remaining time. Side effects additionally need a stable operation identity and a way to resolve outcomes that may already have committed. || Parent deadline established / Attempt consumes remaining time / Backoff includes jitter / Stop when budget or safe retry condition ends

A report takes minutes to generate. Instead of holding the initial request open, the server can return an operation resource that the client polls or subscribes to. Required progress must be stored beyond the request's memory. ¶ A streaming API returns a sequence of messages and needs framing, buffer limits, cancellation, and possibly a resume policy. Decide whether disconnecting cancels the business job or only the connection. A slow reader should not accidentally hold an unbounded database transaction. Use bounded pages or an explicitly owned streaming resource. The interface must explain how work is tracked after the initiating client has gone away. || Submit operation / Persist job and return operation ID / Worker advances durable state / Client observes or cancels under contract

A payment service sends a webhook announcing a completed operation. The receiver commits its local effect, but the success reply is lost. The sender retries, so the same event arrives again. ¶ Give webhooks stable event identifiers and a documented signature and timestamp-verification policy. The receiver should persist the required inbox or effect before acknowledging, then recognise redelivery. The sender needs bounded retries and a recovery policy. A 2xx reply establishes only what the receiver's contract promises; it does not mean every later downstream action is complete. Treat the acknowledgment as a named step in the delivery protocol. || Sender signs event E / Receiver verifies and stores E / Receiver acknowledges receipt / Lost acknowledgment causes duplicate delivery

A restored consumer replays last year's events. It encounters fields and status values no current request uses. Deleting the old decoder can therefore break recovery even if today's API traffic works. ¶ Schema evolution applies to events and stored records as well as live requests. Preserve field meanings, defaults, version identity, and handling of unknown values. Test historical samples and any transformations used to read them. An extra field can also affect signatures or uniqueness rules, so “additive” does not automatically mean harmless. Plan compatibility for the full life of the data, including replay, backup restoration, and delayed clients. || Historical event version 1 / New decoder supports old meaning / Version 2 adds explicit field / Replay validates both histories

A consumer expects a payment status to be either pending or final. A provider test that checks only “status is a string” misses that assumption. Consumer-driven contracts record what actual clients need from their provider. ¶ Run relevant provider checks before releasing a change, including representative errors and state behaviour. Keep the contracts focused on meaningful obligations rather than incidental JSON formatting. Schema tests alone cannot establish retry safety, authorisation, or pagination correctness. Use concrete histories for these behaviours. The contract is useful when it protects a real dependency while still allowing the provider to change internal implementation details. || Consumer states required behavior / Provider verifies contract / Release checks compatible semantics / Runtime telemetry catches unmodeled behavior

A mobile screen needs three small pieces of data from several services. A Backend for Frontend, or BFF, can assemble the response around that client's needs. A gateway usually handles shared edge concerns, such as routing and coarse admission. ¶ Both add operating work and can become bottlenecks. Keep ownership checks in services that know the resource rules, and give the aggregation layer a clear owner. A universal BFF that every product must edit can reduce independence. If results are cached, define the key, tenant scope, and freshness. Aggregating private results does not make them safe for a shared public cache. || Mobile client requests screen data / BFF aggregates bounded calls / Gateway applies edge policy / Domain services enforce ownership

Two internal services may release at different times and communicate over a network. Their API therefore still has compatibility, trust, load, and failure concerns. “Internal” does not mean that every request is valid or authorised. ¶ A public API usually needs longer compatibility commitments, discoverability, abuse protection, and support processes. State the intended audience and lifetime of both kinds. Internal interfaces can become important dependencies whose accidental behaviour is difficult to change. Document their meaningful guarantees early, and apply proportionate authentication, validation, and resource limits instead of relying solely on location inside a network. || Identify API audience / Define compatibility duration / Enforce identity and capacity policy / Observe consumers before changing behavior

A developer needs to create a payment and recover if the reply is lost. Documentation that shows only a successful request leaves the most consequential decision unexplained. ¶ Include complete examples for validation errors, access denial, pagination, operation keys, timeouts, and pending states. A machine-readable schema can generate client types, while prose explains the behaviour those types cannot express. Keep examples aligned with the implementation and test the important ones. A sandbox must clearly distinguish simulated effects from real charges. Good discoverability means a reader can find the operation, understand its scope, and choose a supported recovery action when the usual sequence is interrupted. || Find operation / Read schema and behavioral contract / Try safe example / Follow documented recovery path''')
